*** Add File: source/services/decision-shadow/README.md
+# Decision shadow pilot
+
+This module runs optional OpenRouter Decisions comparisons for two root-agent events:
+
+- which executable capability best matches the current request; and
+- how to triage a terminal provider failure.
+
+The comparisons are observation-only. They do not select tools, change retries, approve actions, or alter foreground settlement.
+
+## Opt in
+
+Set `agent.decisionShadowModel` to an OpenRouter Decisions model. The setting is read for every observation, so changing it applies to the next request. When it is unset or blank, the pilot makes no Decisions request.
+
+The root `AgentClient` uses `agent.openrouter.apiKey`, falling back to `OPENROUTER_API_KEY`, and the optional `agent.openrouter.baseUrl`. Agent-override clients used by subagents do not create the pilot.
+
+## Data sent and logged
+
+Tool-selection requests send the final provider request input plus a snapshot of the capabilities that were executable for that turn. The catalog includes direct tools and the ready, collision-filtered built-in and MCP tools exposed through `run_code`, with their descriptions, schemas, effect classification, and approval capability.
+
+Failure-triage requests send bounded runtime evidence: provider and model identifiers, service tier, HTTP status or provider code when available, retry-after, a clipped error message, and error/cause names. Runtime classifier category, retryability, cancellation classification, and observed outcome are not prediction state. They are attached only to the later comparison log.
+
+Successful comparisons log one of:
+
+- `decision_shadow.tool_selection`: prompt version, requested/resolved shadow model, latency, provider-reported cost in USD micros or the literal `unknown`, predicted capability, confidence, and the later observed selection/outcome;
+- `decision_shadow.failure_triage`: the same request metadata plus predicted category/retry and the separately recorded runtime comparison labels.
+
+Skipped and failed observations use `decision_shadow.skipped` and `decision_shadow.failed`. This module does not log the request input, tool schemas, or raw provider response, although those values are sent to the configured OpenRouter endpoint.
+
+## Replay
+
+`replay-fixtures.ts` keeps expected labels outside evidence and `evaluateReplayFixtures()` reuses the live prompt builders and parsers. The deterministic replay contract can be run without network access:
+
+```bash
+pnpm test source/services/decision-shadow/replay-evaluator.test.ts
+```
+
+A model evaluation runner can import `evaluateReplayFixtures`, provide a `DecisionClient`, a model, and fixture arrays, then record `total`, `correct`, and any per-fixture request or parse errors.
+
+## Limits
+
+- Tool selection predicts one primary or first logical capability, not an arbitrary multi-tool plan. Nested `run_code` calls are compared as their logical built-in or MCP selections when execution evidence is available.
+- Synthetic fixtures exercise the evaluator contract; they are not a model-quality benchmark or production ground truth.
+- One pilot-wide admission cap allows four concurrent tool-selection and failure-triage requests. Completed tool predictions waiting for a foreground outcome are also bounded to four. A fifth observation is skipped rather than queued.
+- The shared OpenRouter Decisions transport aborts each request after 10 seconds. Timeout, malformed response, missing credentials, and logging failures are contained and cannot affect the foreground request.
+- Observed outcomes and runtime failure classifications are comparison labels, not evidence that the prediction was objectively correct.
